(Bug #65's other half. Every sentence the fix now refuses has a documented
 way to say what it meant, and both ways answer correctly. The refused
 spellings are tests/compile_fail/145-157; this is what those programs
 become, and what they print is the answer their authors were after. The
 number/float family at the foot is the opposite case: not refused at all,
 by the language designer's ruling, and now answering correctly.)

(`a text called n is 5.` segfaulted. Either declare the type the value has...)
a number called n is 5.
print n.
(...or convert, LANGUAGE.md:1908.)
a text called spelled is 5 as text.
print spelled.

(`a number called x is "get five".` printed 4198488 - the literal's address,
 which is LANGUAGE.md:651-660's worked example almost to the digit.)
a text called phrase is "get five".
print phrase.
a number called five is "5" as a number.
print five.

(The explicit casts between the two numeric types, LANGUAGE.md:1906-1907,
 which say the conversion out loud and always did.)
a float called widened is 3 as a float.
print widened.
a number called truncated is 3.5 as a number.
print truncated.

(`a boolean called ready is "x".` printed a pointer. LANGUAGE.md:1917 parses
 the text instead.)
a boolean called ready is "true" as a boolean.
print ready.

(`a text called label is true.` segfaulted on address 1. LANGUAGE.md:1916
 spells the boolean out.)
a text called label is true as text.
print label.

(`a list called items is 5.` walked a list header at address 5; a list is
 written as a list.)
a list called items is [5].
print items.

(`a map called ages is "bo".` printed an empty map that was never a map.)
a map called ages is {"bo": 42}.
print ages.

(The three sites 157-159 refuse - a call's result, an argument, a return -
 each written so the types agree.)
To 'five' . Return a number, 5.
To greet with a text called who. Print who.
To 'give a label' . Return a text, "five".

a text called got is five as text.
print got.
greet with 5 as text.
a text called given is 'give a label'.
print given.

(The language designer's ruling (Josj, 2026-08-21): "in human language we
 call 1 a number and pi a number; it should be the same in Vox - dynamic
 casting as and when needed". So neither direction is refused. A whole
 number initialising a float is converted to one at the declaration - this
 printed 0.0 before, the integer's bits read as a double - and a float
 initialising a number keeps its decimals, exactly as it always did.)
a float called ratio is 3.
print ratio.
a float called summed is 3 add 4.
print summed.
a number called count is 3.5.
print count.

(And the float really is a float afterwards, so arithmetic on it is right.
 `ratio multiply 2.0` printed 0.0 before.)
print ratio multiply 2.0.
